{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And if the Jubilee should be, etc.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained (Yerushalmi Ketubot 9:1) that 'if' means 'even if,' meaning: even when the Jubilee comes, in any event this field shall not go out in the Jubilee. But it is forced to explain 'if' in the sense of 'even though.' And if you were to say that it is indeed so, it is in any case difficult: from where would one arrive at the notion that the Jubilee would be a remedy for this field? If so, what did you suppose, and what is it informing us? And furthermore one may press on the verses, for it says: ״וְנִגְרְעָה נַחֲלָתָן מִנַּחֲלַת אֲבוֹתֵינוּ וְנוֹסַף עַל נַחֲלַת הַמַּטֶּה אֲשֶׁר תִּהְיֶינָה לָהֶם וּמִגּוֹרַל נַחֲלָתֵנוּ יִגָּרֵעַ״ — why do I need two instances of 'יִגָּרֵעַ'? And why did they first call it 'נַחֲלַת אֲבוֹתֵינוּ' and afterward call it 'נַחֲלָתֵנוּ,' and afterward turn back and say ״וְאִם יִהְיֶה הַיּוֹבֵל וְגוֹ׳ וּמִנַּחֲלַת מַטֵּה אֲבוֹתֵינוּ יִגָּרֵעַ״? Is not this variation a matter [requiring explanation]? And furthermore it is difficult: why should their inheritance be diminished, when it is written (above, 33:54): ״לָרַב תַּרְבּוּ וְלַמְעַט תַּמְעִיטוּ״?" "And what seems closest to me to say is that they were concerned about two matters. The first is this: that if the lot should go out by the Holy Spirit that this territory should belong to such-and-such a tribe, it is fitting that the name of the deceased, who is the head of the tribe, be called upon his inheritance — for here the dead inherited from the living; if so, it is fitting that the name of the deceased not be cut off from his inheritance. And concerning this apprehension they said: ״וּמִנַּחֲלַת מַטֵּה אֲבוֹתֵינוּ יִגָּרֵעַ״. The second apprehension is because of the suspicion that people might say they sold their inheritance, for later generations would say: what business does another tribe have with the fields of this tribe? For not everyone is expert in the laws of the daughters of Zelophehad, and furthermore perhaps the women will die and their husbands will inherit them, and people will say: surely because of their straits they sold their fields, and they will hold them to be sinners — as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in parashat Behar from the juxtaposition of the parashiyot: one who sells his field — his sins caused this (Kiddushin 20a). And concerning this apprehension they said: ״וּמִגּוֹרַל נַחֲלָתֵנוּ יִגָּרֵעַ״ — it is not an actual diminishment, but rather on account of the suspicion." "It is specifically before the Jubilee that there is room for these two apprehensions, but if the Jubilee should come, then there is no room for the second apprehension, for no one would say that their fields were sold — for if a field had been sold it would revert in the Jubilee, and since this field does not revert in the Jubilee, by this everyone would know that this is not a sold field but rather the daughters transferred this inheritance to another tribe, and one apprehension is nullified. But in any event the second apprehension — as he labels it, actually referring to the first — stands in its place: that from the inheritance of the tribal portion of our fathers it would be diminished, that the name of the deceased would not be called upon his inheritance. And in this manner this 'if' will be like every 'if' found throughout the entire Torah."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {1 "In comment [1] the Kli Yakar appears to refer back to the first apprehension with the phrase 'the second apprehension,' which implies a sequence; I have preserved his exact ordering though he labels them first and second but then refers back as 'this apprehension' and 'the second apprehension.'", 2 "The author's final reference to 'the second apprehension' appears to mislabel what he had earlier called the first apprehension (concerning the name of the deceased); I have translated his words literally while inserting a clarifying note in the body, since the logic of the passage makes clear he means the first apprehension, which he identified with the verse וּמִנַּחֲלַת מַטֵּה אֲבוֹתֵינוּ יִגָּרֵעַ."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:18.361755571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}